WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.590
Manchester Airport is the third largest airport
in the UK

00:00:10.879 --> 00:00:14.190
we have two runways, three terminals

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:17.460
handling over twenty-three million passengers
a year

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.599
So there could be a variety of issues that
we deal with.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.230
So I got involved with the Airport Optimization
Project

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.679
through the Engineering and Physical Sciences
Research Council Sandpit

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.699
We talked about the issues that we have

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:37.580
and we set them to task of coming up with
identifying solutions.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.730
Taxiing is important because it links together
the other problems at an airport

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:44.550
so you have the order in which aircraft take
off and land

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.149
you have where they go at the terminal building,
the gates and taxiing links these two things

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:49.649
together.

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:52.190
The ground movement is particularly important

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.649
because if you can get a better prediction
of taxi times

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:57.870
you can better predict when aircraft are going
to arrive at the runway.

00:00:58.199 --> 00:01:00.949
Because of that you can actually hold them
on stands for a bit longer

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.600
If you can hold them on stands for a bit longer,
you can start their engines a bit later

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.100
Which actually gets you much better pollution
benefits as well as financial cost benefits,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.330
because you burn less fuel.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.030
We need to try and solve the problem using
optimization techniques.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.340
Understandably airports are reluctant to share
their data with just anybody

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.439
I started looking for ways to try and get
data about taxiing at

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:27.520
a whole bunch of different airports with different
characteristics around the world.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:34.259
One of the things cloud computing does is
it brings the power and the data processing

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:34.939
ability

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.630
of huge machines to any researchers desk.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.960
I'm really positive about Azure, I think it's
a great platform.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:47.070
What it gave me access to was a scalable,
powerful machine that I could use to do the

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:47.930
job that I wanted.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.240
Part of this project, I have written three
open source tools available on GitHub

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:57.700
and they are SnapTracks, TaxiGen and GM2KML

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.890
and these all take care of different aspects
of processing data connected with airport

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.219
ground movement or taxiing.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.899
I was pleantly surprised by how simple it
was to use open source tools with Azure

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:11.899
Linux, OpenJDK, SSH all worked just straight away very easily.

00:02:15.859 --> 00:02:19.070
The computing power that we've got now has
allowed us to analyze data

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:22.320
in different ways and to pull out different
pieces of information.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.590
So we can actually understand a lot better
the true uncertainty in taxiing

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.730
so we can understand which aircraft take a
long time to get there, which aircraft get

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:32.720
there very quickly

00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:37.579
and we can understand under what circumstances
this is happening, which means we can optimize

00:02:37.579 --> 00:02:38.579
the system as a whole.

00:02:38.949 --> 00:02:42.579
As passengers we all have experienced various
delays at airports

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.320
and if those delays can be reduced by more
efficient operations obviously that benefits

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:47.840
us all.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.739
Azure really made this research possible in
a reasonable time frame.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.650
I had quite a big amount of data that I wanted
to be able to get through

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.780
and I was able to process it in something
like a tenth of the time I would have done

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.709
on my desktop

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:05.959
So rather than spending several months waiting
for my data to be ready, I had it within a

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:06.850
couple weeks.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:12.720
Aviation is an industry that's growing, so
there are lots of ways that the industry

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:18.100
is trying to tackle the impact that growth
could bring to the climate.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:24.260
So the Airport Optimization Project feeds
into that because we want to reduce the amount

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.880
of CO2 that's generated

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.579
when an aircraft lands, taxis and ultimately
departs from the airport.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:35.220
The Airport Optimization Project is going
to be really important

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:41.099
to make sure that we maintain the efficiency,
safety and passenger experience.

